        "faq_too_much": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "q": "Can you take too many vitamins?",
            "a": "<p>Yes, taking excessive amounts of certain vitamins, particularly fat-soluble vitamins like A and D, can lead to unwanted build-up because your body stores them. Water-soluble vitamins are usually flushed out in urine when consumed in excess, but very high doses can still cause mild digestive discomfort. Always follow the recommended daily intake on the product label and check with your healthcare professional before starting high-dose supplements.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_water_vs_fat": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "q": "What is the difference between water-soluble and fat-soluble vitamins?",
            "a": "<p>Water-soluble vitamins (Vitamin C and the eight B-complex vitamins) dissolve in water and are not stored in significant amounts by your body, meaning they require regular intake through diet or daily supplementation. Fat-soluble vitamins (Vitamins A, D, E, and K) are absorbed alongside dietary fats and stored in the liver and fatty tissues for future use.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_best_time": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "q": "When is the best time of day to take vitamin supplements?",
            "a": "<p>Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) are best absorbed when taken with a meal containing healthy dietary fats, such as breakfast or lunch. Water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) can be taken with water in the morning to support daytime energy release. Taking your supplements alongside a meal also helps prevent mild stomach discomfort.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_natural_vs_synthetic": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "q": "Are natural food sources better than synthetic vitamins?",
            "a": "<p>Whole foods provide a rich mix of vitamins, minerals, fibre, and natural phytonutrients that work together. Supplementation helps fill dietary gaps when whole food intake falls short or when absorption is impaired. High-quality supplements use bioavailable forms that your body can easily process alongside a balanced diet.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_who_should_supplement": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "q": "Who benefits most from taking daily vitamin supplements?",
            "a": "<p>Daily vitamin supplementation is particularly beneficial for individuals with busy lifestyles, adults over 50, pregnant or nursing women, people following vegan or vegetarian diets, and those with limited sun exposure. Speak to your healthcare professional to identify your specific nutritional needs.</p>"
          }
        }
